																		Junior Staff - Site Criticism
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/tanhony" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(776854); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=776854&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606834648" alt="Tanhony" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=776854)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/tanhony" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(776854); return false;" >Tanhony</a></span> <span class="odate time_1567022371 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">28 Aug 2019 19:59</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4343062">
									

<p>I really loved this.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Tanhony&nbsp;rambles</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Tanhony&nbsp;stops&nbsp;rambling</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Anomalous history is one of my favourite themes on the site, and this was pulled off really well - you really get the feeling that this is the Foundation working backwards to dig up and analyze events from long ago without actually having access to those first hand sources.</p>
<p>I was already on board from the premise of a historical event being designated as an SCP just from how damn weird it was, and as I read on and you just went into more and more detail (especially with the descriptions of the various anomalous soldiers and phonomena) I just got more and more hooked. Tying in all the weird medieval art as being part of the records of this conflict was a lovely touch, as well. You’ve got me intrigued with the mentions of the grail and the ark, too - excuse me if I’m wrong, but those felt like definite hooks for future stuff, and I’d love to see more in this style.</p>
<p>One little touch I really appreciated was:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In the letters of Joshua the Clean (Footnote: A soldier that fought alongside Raymond the V in 1218 and died in 1220.)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It’s a really small detail, but just going into that tiny bit of description about the soldier who recorded the knowledge makes it feel like real history beyond just the confines of the article and lends it a lot of verisimilitude.</p>
<p>One thing I’m not 100% sure on is the use of the <em>Sæfernmúða Edlæcan</em>. The intiial mention of it and the actual transcript are pretty far apart, so it’s easy to forget about that specific anomalous device after it’s first mentioned and get confused as to how the Foundation got this transcript. Maybe have another mention of it a little later, just before the transcript, to keep it in the reader’s mind? It’s a pretty tiny nitpick, all things considered, but it’s just something that I thought I should mention.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Still though, definite +1 from me!</p>

							</div>

								
